#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Overview {#overview}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"With SQOA Solver by JIJ, you can solve large combinatorial optimization "
"problems on IBM&reg; quantum computers. The solver is based on the Sampling-"
"based Quantum Optimization Algorithm with Quantum Relaxation (SQOA-QR) "
"[\\[1\\]](#references). Unlike quantum algorithms that use a one-to-one "
"mapping between classical decision variables and qubits, SQOA Solver can "
"handle optimization problems with more variables than the number of qubits "
"available on current quantum devices. Its sampling-based design also makes "
"it less sensitive to the physical noise on current quantum hardware. "
"Together, these features make SQOA Solver well suited to optimization "
"problems at this stage of quantum computing.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The solver handles Quadratic Unconstrained Binary Optimization (QUBO) "
"problems given as a classical mathematical formulation, and it automatically"
" maps the classical formulation into the quantum setting. You can also "
"convert constrained problems into unconstrained ones by using the penalty "
"method.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The following sections describe the solver's workflow and benchmark results."
" The [get started guide](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/functions/jij-sqoa-"
"solver/get-started) shows end-to-end examples that model problems with "
"[qiskit-addon-opt-mapper](https://github.com/Qiskit/qiskit-opt-mapper) and, "
"optionally, Open Mathematical prograMming eXchange (OMMX). "
"[OMMX](https://ommx-en-book.readthedocs.io/en/latest/) is an open data "
"format and SDK that simplifies the exchange of optimization problems between"
" software tools.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Description {#description}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"SQOA Solver implements the Sampling-based Quantum Optimization Algorithm "
"with Quantum Relaxation (SQOA-QR) and surrounds it with classical "
"preprocessing and postprocessing. This section explains the SQOA framework "
"and how it differs from the Quantum Approximate Optimization Algorithm "
"(QAOA).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The following figure shows a schematic of the SQOA Solver workflow.\n"
"![Schematic of the SQOA Solver workflow, from classical preprocessing through SQOA sampling to postprocessing and local search](/docs/images/guides/jij-sqoa-solver/sqoa-workflow.svg)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The SQOA framework {#the-sqoa-framework}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"SQOA is a sampling-based framework for combinatorial optimization "
"[\\[1\\]](#references). It combines two techniques: Quantum Random Access "
"Optimization (QRAO) [\\[2\\]](#references), which encodes the problem in a "
"quantum relaxation, and sample-based quantum diagonalization (SQD) "
"[\\[3\\]](#references), also known as quantum-selected configuration "
"interaction (QSCI) [\\[4\\]](#references), which uses the quantum computer "
"only to sample bitstrings and finds the low-energy state on a classical "
"computer. SQOA is a framework in the sense that it fixes this combination "
"but leaves the details to each implementation, such as how the variables are"
" assigned to qubits and which circuits generate the samples.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**Quantum relaxation with QRAO.** QAOA and other Ising-based approaches "
"represent each binary variable by the $Z$ operator of its qubit. QRAO also "
"uses the $X$ and $Y$ operators. With a (3,1) quantum random access code "
"(QRAC), up to three binary variables share one qubit, and each variable is "
"represented by a different Pauli operator.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  The QUBO then becomes a relaxed Hamiltonian that needs fewer qubits than "
"the Ising Hamiltonian. Variables that interact in the QUBO must be placed on"
" different qubits. If they shared a qubit, the product of their Pauli "
"operators would reduce to a single operator on that qubit (for example, $XY "
"= iZ$), and the relaxed Hamiltonian would no longer represent their "
"interaction.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  The assignment of variables to qubits therefore determines both the number of qubits and the quality of the relaxation, and SQOA Solver designs this assignment in its preprocessing.\n"
"- **Sample-based quantum diagonalization (SQD).** The quantum computer first prepares a quantum state that overlaps with low-energy states of the relaxed Hamiltonian and then samples bitstrings from it. A classical computer projects the relaxed Hamiltonian onto the subspace spanned by the sampled bitstrings and diagonalizes it to approximate the ground state.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  Because the relaxed Hamiltonian is non-diagonal, the diagonalization "
"combines the sampled bitstrings into a superposition. With a diagonal Ising "
"Hamiltonian, every bitstring is already an eigenstate, and the "
"diagonalization would only select the best sample. The same approach has "
"been applied to large-scale problems in quantum chemistry.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The approximate ground state obtained by SQD does not directly correspond to"
" a classical solution of the original problem, so the classical solution "
"must be decoded from it. Because this state is computed on a classical "
"computer, the decoding, called rounding, is classical postprocessing. SQOA "
"Solver uses decoding techniques designed to obtain better classical "
"solutions heuristically.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The following table compares SQOA with QAOA, a common variational approach "
"to the same class of problems.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"| &nbsp; | QAOA | SQOA |\n"
"|---|---|---|\n"
"| Qubits for $n$ binary variables | $n$ | One qubit holds up to three of the variables. The exact number depends on the problem structure |\n"
"| Use of the QPU | Repeatedly estimates the energy of parameterized circuits inside a classical optimization loop | Only samples bitstrings. No circuit parameters are trained on the QPU |\n"
"| From samples to a solution | Selects the best measured bitstring | Diagonalizes the relaxed Hamiltonian in the sampled subspace on a classical computer and rounds the resulting state |\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Around SQOA-QR, SQOA Solver adds classical processing. Its preprocessing "
"fixes variables whose optimal values can be determined in advance and "
"designs the assignment of variables to qubits. Its postprocessing refines "
"the rounded solution with a local search, which you can turn off with "
"`postprocessing_level=0`.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Benchmarks {#benchmarks}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Admonition type=\"caution\" title=\"Disclaimer\">\n"
"  Performance can depend on both the problem instance and subsequent processing steps. In some cases, classical samples and quantum-generated samples might achieve similar final solution quality after equivalent postprocessing. Evaluation should therefore consider the complete optimization workflow.\n"
"</Admonition>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The following benchmark metrics provide a general indication of solution "
"quality, qubit compression, and execution time across selected problem sizes"
" and structures. The table lists a selection of [Maximum Independent Set "
"instances](https://github.com/ZIB-AOPT/QOBLIB/tree/main/07-independentset) "
"from the Quantum Optimization Benchmarking Library (QOBLIB) and two "
"3-regular MaxCut instances. You can load the QOBLIB instances as "
"`ommx.v1.Instance` objects, together with their optimal solutions, by using "
"the [ommx-quantum-benchmarks](https://jij-"
"inc.github.io/OmmxQuantumBenchmarks/) package.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Each instance was run 10 times on an IBM Heron r2 processor. `Number of "
"qubits` counts the qubits actually used on hardware after preprocessing and "
"QRAC encoding. The compression ratio is instance-dependent. `Approximation "
"ratio` (AR) reports the quality of the best solution found over the 10 "
"runs:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\mathrm{AR} = \\frac{C_{\\textrm{best}}}{C_{\\textrm{opt}}},\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"where $C_{\\textrm{best}}$ is the best objective value found and "
"$C_{\\textrm{opt}}$ is the known optimal objective value. All instances are "
"maximization problems with a positive optimal value, so AR is at most 100%, "
"and 100% in the `Approximation ratio` column indicates that the solver found"
" an optimal solution. `Total time` is the average combined QPU and CPU time "
"per run. `QPU time` is the average IBM Quantum Compute usage per run.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"| Instance | Number of variables | Number of qubits | Approximation ratio | Total time (seconds) | QPU time (seconds) |\n"
"|---|:---:|:---:|:---:|:---:|:---:|\n"
"| maxcut_384_nodes | 384 | 129 | 98.5% | 62.1 | 12.0 |\n"
"| gen200_p0-9_44 | 200 | 67 | 100% | 38.0 | 12.0 |\n"
"| es60fst02 | 186 | 42 | 100% | 24.7 | 12.0 |\n"
"| es60fst04 | 162 | 29 | 100% | 17.9 | 12.0 |\n"
"| maxcut_150_nodes | 150 | 51 | 100% | 23.1 | 12.0 |\n"
"| sloane_1dc_128 | 128 | 37 | 93.8% | 19.1 | 12.0 |\n"
"| sloane_2dc_128 | 128 | 8 | 100% | 12.3 | 11.5 |\n"
"| C125-9 | 125 | 43 | 100% | 22.3 | 12.0 |\n"
"| es60fst01 | 123 | 12 | 100% | 13.6 | 12.0 |\n"
"| es60fst03 | 113 | 26 | 100% | 14.9 | 12.0 |\n"
"| sloane_1dc_64 | 64 | 16 | 100% | 13.4 | 11.9 |\n"
"| chesapeake | 39 | 13 | 100% | 13.1 | 12.0 |\n"
"| football | 35 | 6 | 100% | 12.5 | 12.0 |\n"
"| karate | 34 | 2 | 100% | 6.2 | 6.0 |\n"
"| ibm32 | 32 | 11 | 100% | 12.9 | 12.0 |\n"
"| farm | 17 | 4 | 100% | 8.5 | 8.0 |\n"
msgstr ""

